{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q007_chey_like_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe places these domains in the CheY-like superfamily (c.23.1), within the flavodoxin-like fold (c.23) of the α/β class. Their shared superfamily supports inferred common ancestry despite substantial functional divergence; it does not establish one functional family or interchangeable activities. The relationship concerns the classified domains, not necessarily the complete proteins.

CheY-related response-regulator receiver domains commonly mediate phosphorylation-dependent signaling, but this does not make every member a chemotaxis regulator. Neither the ornithine-decarboxylase N-terminal wing nor the KaiA N-terminal domain can be assigned canonical receiver-domain aspartyl phosphorylation, two-component signaling, or flagellar-motor binding from this classification. Ornithine decarboxylase’s PLP-dependent conversion of ornithine to putrescine cannot be transferred to CheY or KaiA, nor attributed to the isolated wing merely because it belongs to that enzyme. KaiA’s role in regulating the KaiC circadian phosphorylation cycle likewise does not make its N-terminal domain a kinase or establish that this domain alone stimulates KaiC.

These relatives can inform structural comparisons, but functional templates or representatives must be chosen using the relevant family, conserved functional residues, and domain architecture. Specific signaling or catalytic assignments require independent biochemical evidence, such as phosphorylation, interaction, or enzyme-activity measurements.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
